# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git for Windows ships with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this file a
# fresh clone on Windows rewrites every text file to CRLF in the working
# tree. For most files that is harmless. For the scripts that run *inside a
# Linux container* it is fatal:
#
#     /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: bad interpreter:
#     /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory
#
# That breaks `docker compose up` for anyone who clones on Windows, while
# working perfectly for whoever authored it — the worst kind of bug to ship.
#
# `eol=lf` pins LF in the working tree on every platform, which is what the
# shebang, the Dockerfiles and the shell scripts need.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Called out explicitly, because these are the ones that actually break.
*.sh                 text eol=lf
Dockerfile           text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
*.mako               text eol=lf

# Tab-indented by necessity; LF as well.
Makefile             text eol=lf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.pdf    binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# Generated lockfiles: keep them out of review diffs and resolve conflicts by
# regenerating rather than by editing.
uv.lock           -diff linguist-generated
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
